{# W3C SCXML 4.9: "If the processing of an element causes an error to be
   raised, the processor MUST NOT process the remaining elements of the
   block." The statement that ends the block an element sits in, written once
   for every element that can raise:

     - inside a <foreach> body, which runs in `executeForeach`'s lambda that a
       `return` cannot leave: throw `ActionBlockAbort`, which the <foreach>
       catches without raising again and then ends its own block;
     - inside an entry, exit or <initial> block, each of which is its own
       `run {}`: `return@run`;
     - otherwise (transition content, history default content, each one
       function call running exactly one block): `return`.

   The caller passes the two facts, because an imported macro does not see
   the includer's context. The statement must sit OUTSIDE any lambda of the
   element's own, or `return@run` would leave that lambda instead of the
   block — which is why an element that needs one writes
   `if (run name@{ ... }) { <this> }`. #}
{% macro stmt(in_foreach_body, in_run_block) -%}
{% if in_foreach_body %}throw com.sce.runtime.ActionBlockAbort(){% elif in_run_block %}return@run{% else %}return{% endif %}
{%- endmacro %}
